## Orchestrator Role

You **MUST NOT** dispatch `Pl*` task cards without
first asking me. You own dispatch for all other
types of cards.

You **MUST** always `start` `Hu*` cards before
any other dispatch if `ready`, and you SHOULD
always interactively streamline them to me
for decisions using the `AskUserQuestion`
tool. The planner expects you to log all
of my decisions into the cards as I give them
to you.

You **MUST** wait for me to close `Hu` task
cards; you cannot close them yourself by design.

You **MUST** give all code-writing agents a
one liner directive to read `./STYLE.md` in
FULL before doing anything else.

You SHOULD prefer asking the USER for
clarity over guessing. Bad assumptions
poison all downstream agents and workstreams.

You SHOULD make use of `AskUserQuestion`
to distill and streamline my attention
by default. I will let you know if deeper
discussion is merited.

You SHOULD run `goof tasks ready` after
each `goof tasks close <card>` to check
if anything new was unblocked.

### Working Tree Safety

You **MUST NOT** run `git commit`, `git stash`,
`git checkout`, `git restore`, or any command
that mutates the working tree while implementer
or contractor agents are in flight. These agents
write directly to the working tree; stashing or
committing under them corrupts their output and
wastes their entire context window.

If a pre-commit hook fails because an inflight
agent left a broken intermediate state, **wait**
for all agents to finish, then commit. Do not
work around it with stash.

### Code Review and Commit

You **MUST** follow all `implementer` and
`contractor` agents with a `correctness-reviewer`
and `style-reviewer` agent in parallel. In any
security relevant context, you **MUST** also
dispatch a `security-reviewer` in parallel.

You **MUST** give pointers to any reviewing
agent to the original `Pl` planner card AND
any in-scope implementation task cards. Do NOT
inject card content into the agents context;
agents SHOULD read them directly.

You **MUST** flag all security issues to me
for decision; you SHOULD fix all other non-
security findings from `reviewer` agents
yourself. Finally, after all outstanding
findings are resolved, you should commit the
self-contained code and close any associated
upstream issues using `ghfa`.

Each specialized reviewer reads its own
reference doc (`STYLE.md`, `SECURITY.md`)
on startup; do not inject them.

Commits SHOULD map one-to-one with an implementing
and reviewing agent finishing. Sometimes there are
minor overlaps; to avoid complexities, you can commit
multiple verified cards' code together; however,
this SHOULD be the exception, not the rule.

### Commit Output Style

You **MUST** write `git` commits in the format:

```
internal/package: a short summary line with less than 10 words

This is an optional prose paragraph
with intentional line breaking that
is used to describe a naunce.

Sometimes a change is complex enough
to merit a secondary prose paragraph
to ensure the context of the change
remains well understood as the code
evolves.
```

You **MUST** include ALL numeric literals in
the `issue_refs` field associated with a given
task card when committing:

```
# without issue_refs
internal/package: a short summary line with less than 10 words

# with issue_refs
internal/package: #1 #11 a short summary line with less than 10 words

# two packages
internal/package, cmd/binary: something really interesting
```

If a commit touches 3 or more packages (including
`cmd`), you SHOULD use `all: ` instead of specifying
all of the packages in the commit summary.

## Orchestrator Loop Protocol

After the `planner` returns from its
`Pl` card, you SHOULD execute the `Pl`
campaign using the following loop:

1. Dispatch - fan out agents
2. Reviewer(s) - upon returning, each code-writing agent gets follow on `reviewer` agent(s)
3. Fix - any non-security findings get fixed immediately; security findings HALT the loop
4. Commit - stage and commit the self-contained unit of work
5. (optional) Close Upstream Issue - if upstream issue is complete, use `ghfa` to close it.
6. Repeat - Go to (1).

## Lean Formalization Campaign Conventions
## (folded in from PLAN.md at its retirement, 2026-07-30;
##  the dispatch state of record is Formalize/INDEX)

1. **Writers.** One prover/postdoc per lane, exactly ONE named
   `.lean` file each; read `STYLE.md` in FULL first; never run
   git; no Scratch files; `lakefile.toml` and root aggregators
   (`Proofs/<Lib>.lean`) are orchestrator-owned. Lake targets
   have NO `Proofs.` prefix (`srcDir = "Proofs"`): the module
   for `Proofs/X/Y.lean` is `X.Y`.
2. **Builds.** Always `flock .lake/agent.lock lake build <Module>`
   (untruncated; retry on lock contention), wrapped in the
   memory fence
   `systemd-run --user --scope -p MemoryMax=12G -p MemorySwapMax=0`.
   NEVER `ulimit -v` (breaks lean's olean mmap at startup). A
   cgroup kill means the computation is infeasible: record the
   wall, back off, never retry bigger. Probe kernel walls from
   below. NOTE: `lake build` on a built module REPLAYS cached
   diagnostics — force `lake env lean` for cold trust checks.
3. **Ground truth.** OEIS via `oeis show <seq>` live, never
   model memory. A failed satisfiability/ground check is
   evidence of a WRONG STATEMENT: halt the lane and escalate —
   never weaken, strengthen, or reshape silently (USER hard
   stop). Cards can be stale or internally inconsistent
   (A391599 precedent): corrections are audit-verified against
   primary sources and recorded as binding card notes.
4. **Review.** Every writer return gets vacuity-cop + reviewer
   in PARALLEL (pointers, not content). Orchestrator applies
   non-security fixes itself, rebuilds, then commits — one
   commit per lane, at drain points only, never while a writer
   is mid-file. Reviewer findings are hypotheses: verify before
   applying (dead-`have`s that feed `omega` via context;
   `lt_or_le` does not exist here — `lt_or_ge` is canonical).
5. **Trust policy.** Axioms exactly
   {propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound}; zero native_decide
   in new work (USER decision 2026-07-30, Gnu precedent):
   kernel decide to the measured wall, classification-lemma-
   backed values beyond, omission or disclosed intended-sorry
   archives for the rest.
6. **Novelty.** A lane that PROVES an OEIS conjecture/comment
   gets a consumer literature sweep (KNOWN / LIKELY-KNOWN /
   NO-REFERENCE-FOUND); verdict + attribution wording go into
   the file header before commit. "First recorded proof" at
   most — never "novel" without a sweep. Def layers need no
   sweep (IntComplexity precedent).
7. **Docs.** Writer notes `<Lib>-<Name>.md`, audits
   `review-vacuity-<Name>.md` / `review-style-<Name>.md`,
   sweeps `novelty-<Name>.md`, all under `.tasks/main/docs/`
   (untracked). Halted lanes carry a leading `/- LANE HALTED
   (date, reason) -/` block; the re-dispatched writer replaces
   the whole file.
